{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's defense relies on a 'risk asymmetry' argument that functionally replicates the inductive inference it claims to avoid. By asserting that choosing a less-corroborated theory 'increases the risk of error,' the target implicitly assigns a higher subjective probability of truth (or lower probability of falsity) to the better-corroborated theory for future applications. This smuggles induction back in as a pragmatic proxy: if corroboration provides no grounds for expecting future success (as Popper's anti-induction stance requires), then the claim that one choice is 'riskier' than another is unfounded within the system. The target fails to explain how 'risk' can be assessed without relying on the very uniformity of nature (that past performance indicates future reliability) that Popper rejected.","premise":"If the preference cannot be distinguished from inductive habit when applied to concrete scientific decisions.","premises_essential":["ca278b1e65a9353d4378a4f3125edc456d707388ba7eb50ff9285a0514632a54"],"successor_question":"Can a Popperian define 'risk of error' in theory selection without implicitly assigning a higher probability of future success to the better-corroborated theory?","target":"ca278b1e65a9353d4378a4f3125edc456d707388ba7eb50ff9285a0514632a54"}]}